Our 1e23 Delphi run finished last night with a loss within 0.005 of the projected (preregistered) loss. These projections were based on training models over 100 times smaller (3e20).

Still, there is more work to do as we encountered loss spikes and noticed that our scaling laws are bending. We have some ideas for addressing both issues.

@WilliamBarrHeld: "How far do Marin's scaling laws extrapolate? At least 100x, apparently! Despite spooky spikes, our 1e23 Delphi finished on forecast. The compute-optimal ladder costs ~1e21 FLOPs to train. Good scaling science lets you 'run' this (not tiny) experiment at 1/100th the cost."

New linear attention kernel! Linear Attention is unlocking million-token context windows by reducing computational complexity from O(N^2) to O(N), but software is increasingly bottlenecking the hardware.

@AntLingAGI: "Meet cuLA (CUDA Linear Attention): hand-written kernels using CuTe DSL & CUTLASS C++ to extract maximum performance on NVIDIA GPUs. A drop-in replacement for FLA designed to push hardware to its absolute limits."

The linked GitHub repository provides CUDA kernels for linear attention variants, written in CuTe DSL and CUTLASS C++.

The Google Gemma 4 architecture is very interesting, with each model featuring subtle differences. For example, the small variant uses per layer embedding, while there is no attention scale, which typically divides QK^T by the square root of d. Instead, they perform QK norm and V norm. Additionally, the large variant shares K and V, and the small variant employs aggressive KV cache sharing.

The sliding window sizes of 512 and 1024 are larger than GPT-OSS's 128, and they do not use sinks. Other features include softcapping and applying rope only to part of the dimensions, with different rope theta for the local and global layers.

@osanseviero: "Gemma 4 is here! 31B and 26B A4B for models with impressive intelligence per parameter. E2B and E4B for mobile and IoT. Apache 2.0. Base and IT checkpoints available. Available in AI Studio, Hugging Face, Ollama, Android, and your favorite OS tools. Download it today!"

Very nice blog with some experimental results at scale about QB load balancing. I really love how QB is just a nicer version of the deepseek bias method; the math is nice. I tried to make a visualization to give some intuition about how and what we are optimizing for!

In terms of experimental results, this seems more stable, expert learning seems to be good (see the cosine similarity plot), there's no need for additional auxiliary loss, and the loss is slightly improving.

@classiclarryd: "Researchers' brilliant ideas often get lost in the sea of endless SOTA claims on weak baselines. At Marin, we battle-test ideas in an open arena, where anyone's idea can be promoted to the next hero run. One that recently rose up was @Jianlin_S MoE Quantile Balancing, used in our last 1e22 and ongoing 130B run. Animated visuals of how QB performed are available in the OpenAthena blog."

Curious what's in the PR of almost 1400 kernels? Here we walk through a simple batched GEMM kernel with a tile size of M128, N16, K256. Matrix A is INT4 with a BF16 scaling factor for every 32 elements, and matrix B is BF16. The kernel features three pipeline stages, one CTA MMA, and a static scheduler.

This warp specialized kernel has specific roles: Load A, Load A scaling factor (SF), Load B, Cast A (dequantizing INT4 to BF16), MMA (performing matmul), and Epilogue (performing activation computation). An interesting aspect is that its MMA uses TS mode due to matrix A dequantization requiring CUDA cores, which operate on registers instead of TMEM. Microbenchmarking shows that TS mode has slightly lower throughput due to SMEM bandwidth bottlenecks, and the CUDA core/Tensor Core compute gap also creates bottlenecks. To mitigate these issues, the kernel employs pipelining, similar to what Cursor did.

@cudagdb: "Trtllmgen kernels are now open. Fastest prefill and decode kernels for our target workloads. We wrote these to win InferenceX, MLPerf, and other benchmarks, powering some of today’s top served models. Dive in, learn, use them, or level up your own. Enjoy."

Are you telling me this is diloco for KVCache? We push Prefill/Decode disaggregation beyond a single cluster: cross-datacenter and heterogeneous hardware, unlocking the potential for significantly lower cost per token. This was previously blocked by KV cache transfer overhead. The key enabler is our hybrid model (Kimi Linear), which reduces KV cache size and makes cross-DC Prefill/Decode practical.

Validated on a 20x scaled-up Kimi Linear model, it achieves 1.54× throughput and a 64% reduction in P90 TTFT, directly translating into lower token costs.

@Kimi_Moonshot: "We push Prefill/Decode disaggregation beyond a single cluster: cross-datacenter + heterogeneous hardware, unlocking the potential for significantly lower cost per token. This was previously blocked by KV cache transfer overhead. The key enabler is our hybrid model (Kimi Linear), which reduces KV cache size and makes cross-DC PD practical. Validated on a 20x scaled-up Kimi Linear model: ✅ 1.54× throughput ✅ 64% ↓ P90 TTFT → Directly translating into lower token cost."

